44: /**
45:  * The context of an individual {@link Handler} invocation.
46:  * <p>
47:  * It provides:
48:  * <ul>
49:  * <li>Access the HTTP {@link #getRequest() request} and {@link #getResponse() response}</li>
50:  * <li>Delegation (via the {@link #next} and {@link #insert} family of methods)</li>
51:  * <li>Access to <i>contextual objects</i> (see below)</li>
52:  * <li>Convenience for common handler operations</li>
53:  * </ul>
54:  * </p>
55:  * <h4>Contextual objects</h4>
56:  * <p>
57:  * A context is also a {@link Registry} of objects.
58:  * Arbitrary objects can be "pushed" into the context for use by <i>downstream</i> handlers.
59:  * <p>
60:  * There are some significant contextual objects that drive key infrastructure.
61:  * For example, error handling is based on informing the contextual {@link ratpack.error.ServerErrorHandler} of exceptions.
62:  * The error handling strategy for an application can be changed by pushing a new implementation of this interface into the context that is used downstream.
63:  * <p>
64:  * See {@link #insert(Handler...)} for more on how to do this.
65:  * <h5>Default contextual objects</h5>
66:  * <p>There is also a set of default objects that are made available via the Ratpack infrastructure:
67:  * <ul>
68:  * <li>The effective {@link ratpack.launch.LaunchConfig}</li>
69:  * <li>A {@link ratpack.file.FileSystemBinding} that is the application {@link ratpack.launch.LaunchConfig#getBaseDir()}</li>
70:  * <li>A {@link ratpack.file.MimeTypes} implementation</li>
71:  * <li>A {@link ratpack.error.ServerErrorHandler}</li>
72:  * <li>A {@link ratpack.error.ClientErrorHandler}</li>
73:  * <li>A {@link ratpack.file.FileRenderer}</li>
74:  * <li>A {@link ratpack.server.BindAddress}</li>
75:  * <li>A {@link ratpack.server.PublicAddress}</li>
76:  * <li>A {@link Redirector}</li>
77:  * </ul>
78:  */
