42: /**
43:  * Provides support for HTTP sessions.
44:  * <p>
45:  * This module provides the general session API (see {@link Session}), and a default {@link SessionStore} implementation that stores session data in local memory.
46:  *
47:  * <h3>The session store</h3>
48:  * <p>
49:  * It is expected that most applications will provide alternative bindings for the {@link SessionStore} type, overriding the default.
50:  * This allows arbitrary stores to be used to persist session data.
51:  * <p>
52:  * The default, in memory, implementation stores the data in a {@link Cache}{@code <}{@link AsciiString}, {@link ByteBuf}{@code >}.
53:  * This cache instance is provided by this module and defaults to storing a maximum of 1000 entries, discarding least recently used.
54:  * The {@link #memoryStore} methods are provided to conveniently construct alternative cache configurations, if necessary.
55:  * <h3>Serialization</h3>
56:  * <p>
57:  * Objects must be serialized to be stored in the session.
58:  * The get/set methods {@link SessionData} allow supplying a {@link SessionSerializer} to be used for the specific value.
59:  * For variants of the get/set methods where a serializer is not provided, the implementation of {@link SessionSerializer} bound with Guice will be used.
60:  * The default implementation provided by this module uses Java's in built serialization mechanism.
61:  * Users of this module may choose to override this binding with an alternative serialization strategy.
62:  * <p>
63:  * However, other Ratpack extensions may require session storage any rely on Java serialization.
64:  * For this reason, there is also always a {@link JavaSessionSerializer} implementation available that is guaranteed to be able to serialize any {@link Serializable}
65:  * object (that conforms to the {@link Serializable} contract.
66:  * Users of this module may also choose to override this binding with another implementation (e.g. one based on <a href="https://github.com/EsotericSoftware/kryo">Kryo</a>),
67:  * but this implementation must be able to serialize any object implementing {@link Serializable}.
68:  *
69:  * It is also often desirable to provide alternative implementations for {@link SessionSerializer} and {@link JavaSessionSerializer}.
70:  * The default binding for both types is an implementation that uses out-of-the-box Java serialization (which is neither fast nor efficient).
71:  *
72:  * <h3>Example usage</h3>
