35: /**
36:  * Static utility methods for creating Google Guice based Ratpack infrastructure.
37:  * <p>
38:  * Any non trivial application will require supporting objects for the handler implementations, for persistence for example.
39:  * These supporting objects can be managed by Guice and made available to handlers (either by dependency injection or registry lookup)
40:  * for increased reusability, modularity and testability.
41:  * <p>
42:  * <p>
43:  * The Guice integration is not part of the Ratpack core library, but is available as a separate add on.
44:  * That said, Ratpack is designed to support something like Guice as an integral piece via it's {@link Registry} abstraction.
45:  * Guice is the “official” solution.
46:  * </p>
47:  * <h4>Starting a Guice Ratpack app</h4>
48:  * <p>
49:  * The user entry point for Ratpack applications is the {@link ratpack.launch.HandlerFactory} implementation
50:  * that is given to the {@link ratpack.launch.LaunchConfigBuilder#build(ratpack.launch.HandlerFactory)} method.
51:  * This implementation can use one of the {@code handler(...) } static methods.
52:  * <p>
53:  * Below is a complete example for bootstrapping a Guice based Ratpack application.
54:  * </p>
55:  * <pre class="tested">
56:  * import ratpack.handling.*;
57:  * import ratpack.registry.*;
58:  * import ratpack.guice.*;
59:  * import ratpack.launch.*;
60:  * import ratpack.func.*;
61:  * import com.google.inject.*;
62:  * import javax.inject.*;
63:  *
64:  * // Some service to be dependency injected
65:  * class SomeService {}
66:  *
67:  * // A Guice module that provides the service
68:  * class ServiceModule extends AbstractModule {
69:  *   protected void configure() {
70:  *     bind(SomeService.class);
71:  *   }
72:  * }
73:  *
74:  * // An action that registers the module with the registry, making it part of the application
75:  * class Bindings implements Action&lt;BindingsSpec&gt; {
76:  *   public void execute(BindingsSpec bindings) {
77:  *     bindings.add(new ServiceModule());
78:  *   }
79:  * }
80:  *
81:  * // A handler implementation that is dependency injected
82:  * {@literal @}Singleton
83:  * class InjectedHandler implements Handler {
84:  *   private final SomeService service;
85:  *
86:  *   {@literal @}Inject
87:  *   public InjectedHandler(SomeService service) {
88:  *     this.service = service;
89:  *   }
90:  *
91:  *   public void handle(Context exchange) {
92:  *     // …
93:  *   }
94:  * }
95:  *
96:  * // A chain configurer that adds a dependency injected handler to the chain
97:  * class HandlersBootstrap implements Action&lt;Chain&gt; {
98:  *   public void execute(Chain chain) {
99:  *
100:  *     // The registry in a Guice backed chain can be used to retrieve objects that were bound,
101:  *     // or to create objects that are bound “just-in-time”.
102:  *     Registry&lt;Object&gt; registry = chain.getRegistry();
103:  *     Handler injectedHandler = registry.get(InjectedHandler.class);
104:  *
105:  *     // Add the handler into the chain
106:  *     chain.get("some/path", injectedHandler);
107:  *   }
108:  * }
109:  *
110:  * // A HandlerFactory implementation used to bootstrap the application
111:  * class MyHandlerFactory implements HandlerFactory {
112:  *   public Handler create(LaunchConfig launchConfig) {
113:  *     return Guice.handler(launchConfig, new Bindings(), new HandlersBootstrap());
114:  *   }
115:  * }
116:  *
117:  * // Building a launch config with our handler factory
118:  * LaunchConfig launchConfig = LaunchConfigBuilder.baseDir(new File("appRoot")).build(new MyHandlerFactory());
119:  * </pre>
120:  * <h4>Accessing Guice bound objects in Handlers</h4>
121:  * <p>
122:  * There are two ways to use Guice bound objects in your handler implementations.
123:  * </p>
124:  * <h5>Dependency Injected Handlers</h5>
125:  * <p>
126:  * The {@code handler()} methods used to create a Guice backed application take an {@link Action} that operates on a {@link Chain} instance.
127:  * This chain instance given to this action provides a Guice backed {@link Registry} via its {@link Chain#getRegistry()} method.
128:  * This registry is able to retrieve objects that were explicitly bound (i.e. defined by a module), and bind objects “just in time”.
129:  * This means that it can be used to construct dependency injected {@link Handler} implementations.
130:  * </p>
131:  * <p>
132:  * Simply pass the class of the handler implementation to create a new dependency injected instance of to this method,
133:  * then add it to the chain.
134:  * </p>
135:  * <p>
136:  * See the code above for an example of this.
137:  * </p>
138:  * <h5>Accessing dependencies via context registry lookup</h5>
139:  * <p>
140:  * The {@link ratpack.handling.Context} object that is given to a handler's {@link Handler#handle(ratpack.handling.Context)}
141:  * method is also a registry implementation. In a Guice backed app, Guice bound objects can be retrieved via the {@link Registry#get(Class)} method of
142:  * the context. You can retrieve any bound object via its publicly bound type.
143:  * </p>
144:  * <p>
145:  * However, this will not create “just-in-time” bindings. Only objects that were explicitly bound can be retrieved this way.
146:  * </p>
147:  * <h4>Guice modules as Ratpack “plugins”.</h4>
148:  * <p>
149:  * Add on Ratpack functionality is typically provided via Guice modules.
150:  * For example, the <a href="https://github.com/FasterXML/jackson-databind">Jackson</a> integration for JSON serialisation
151:  * is provided by the {@code ratpack-jackson} add-on which ships a Guice module.
152:  * To use its functionality simply register the module it provides with the {@link BindingsSpec} used to bootstrap the application.
153:  * </p>
154:  * <h4>Groovy Applications</h4>
155:  * <p>
156:  * The Ratpack Groovy add-on provides application modes that automatically incorporate Guice (namely the “Ratpack Script” mode).
157:  * The module registration process is simpler and more convenient in this mode, and there are additional options for obtaining
158:  * Guice bound objects.
